.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt RSEQ 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rseq
.Nd restartable sequences: the processor a thread runs on, and a critical section a preemption aborts
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In linux/rseq.h
.In sys/syscall.h
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn syscall SYS_rseq "struct rseq *rseq" "uint32_t rseq_len" "int flags" "uint32_t sig"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn rseq
registers the calling thread's
.Vt struct rseq ,
an area of its own memory in which the kernel keeps the processor the
thread runs on, and in which the thread names the critical section it is
in, as Linux 6.12's call does.
glibc registers an area in each thread it starts and reads the processor
from it in
.Fn sched_getcpu ;
a library of per-processor data, such as librseq, runs its updates as
critical sections.
.Pp
The area is laid out as Linux's, 32 bytes aligned to 32:
.Bl -tag -width "cpu_id_start"
.It Va cpu_id_start
The processor, offset 0.
.It Va cpu_id
The processor, offset 4, or
.Dv RSEQ_CPU_ID_UNINITIALIZED
.Pq -1
while no area is registered.
.It Va rseq_cs
The address of the critical section's
.Vt struct rseq_cs ,
offset 8, a 64-bit field that the thread sets on entering the section and
the kernel clears.
.It Va flags
Offset 16, which must be 0.
.It Va node_id
The memory node, offset 20: 0, since the machine has one.
.It Va mm_cid
The concurrency ID, offset 24.
.El
.Pp
The kernel writes
.Va cpu_id_start ,
.Va cpu_id ,
.Va node_id
and
.Va mm_cid
on the thread's first return to user mode after the registration, after
each switch of the thread away from its processor, and before a signal
handler runs.
A
.Vt struct rseq_cs
gives the section's
.Va version ,
0,
.Va flags ,
0, the address of its first instruction
.Va start_ip ,
the bytes
.Va post_commit_offset
from there to the instruction after the one that commits, and the address
.Va abort_ip
of its abort handler, which the 32-bit signature
.Fa sig
precedes in memory.
When the thread is switched away from its processor, or takes a signal,
while its instruction pointer lies in
.Bo Va start_ip ,
.Va start_ip No + Va post_commit_offset
.Bc ,
it resumes at
.Va abort_ip ,
or its signal handler returns there, with
.Va rseq_cs
cleared.
A section the kernel cannot read, one that is not valid, with a version,
flags, an abort handler inside it or an address past user space, an
abort handler without the signature before it, and an area that cannot be
written end the thread's process with
.Dv SIGSEGV ,
unless a handler catches it.
.Pp
A registration takes an area of at least 32 bytes aligned to 32.
.Fa flags
of
.Dv RSEQ_FLAG_UNREGISTER
.Pq 1
with the area, length and signature registered removes it, and the area
then reads as an unregistered one: 0 in
.Va cpu_id_start ,
.Va node_id
and
.Va mm_cid ,
and -1 in
.Va cpu_id .
A thread starts without an area, a
.Xr fork 2
child has its parent's, and
.Xr execve 2
removes it.
The auxiliary vector of a new program gives
.Dv AT_RSEQ_FEATURE_SIZE ,
28, the bytes of the area this kernel keeps, and
.Dv AT_RSEQ_ALIGN ,
32.
.Ss Differences from Linux
.Va mm_cid
is the processor's number, which no two threads of an address space that
run at once share and which is below the number of processors, as Linux's
is; Linux also keeps it below the number of the address space's threads.
The kernel has no
.Fn membarrier ,
whose
.Dv MEMBARRIER_CMD_PRIVATE_EXPEDITED_RSEQ
aborts the sections of the other threads of a process.
.Ss System call interface
The system call number is 386 on i386 and 334 on x86-64.
The x86-64 kernel takes the i386 number from a 32-bit program, with the
same area.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
0 on success.
On failure -1 is returned with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Pa tests/limitsprobe.c
registers an area as glibc 2.44 does, reads its processor after a move to
another processor, and sees a section in which the thread signals itself
resume at its abort handler.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EFAULT]"
.It Bq Er EBUSY
The area is registered already.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The area runs past user space, or for an unregistration cannot be
written.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa flags
is neither 0 nor
.Dv RSEQ_FLAG_UNREGISTER ;
the area is shorter than 32 bytes or not aligned to 32; another area or
length is registered; or no area is registered to be removed.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The signature is not the one registered.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr getcpu 2 ,
.Xr sched_setscheduler 2
